# macOS writes these into any directory opened in Finder. They hold nothing but
# per-folder view preferences, so they are noise in every `git status` and worth
# nothing in history.
.DS_Store

# Editors and tools that "help" by leaving a copy behind. Git already has the
# history, so a .bak is a second, worse copy that outlives its reason.
*.bak
*.bak-*
*.bak.*

# Half-written files from interrupted atomic writes. Eleven of these once held
# 6 MB of dead codex state.
*.tmp-*

# pi writes a lock next to the settings file it is updating.
*.lock

# Notes an agent left behind while working something out. Useful for an hour,
# misleading for a year: they are a snapshot of what was true during one
# session, and the conclusions worth keeping were written into the configs and
# their READMEs instead.
.scratch/*
# Including the orchestration tickets. Joel's ruling, 2026-09-05: they are a
# working surface that changes many times an hour, and history of it is noise.
# They stay on disk; what is worth keeping is written into the configs and their
# READMEs, which are tracked.

# Claude Code's per-repo directory. It lives here and is never tracked.
.claude/

# The status bar's compiled probe, and the picture of the spectrum rule. Both
# are build output keyed to this machine -- the rule is sized to this screen --
# and install.sh rebuilds them. Same rule as "Pi Open.app": a binary in a
# dotfiles repo is a thing you cannot read a diff of.
sketchybar/bin/sysprobe
sketchybar/bin/hide-menubar
sketchybar/bin/wake-powerui

# pi-background-tasks spools job output and delegate/Fusion artifacts under the
# cwd, keyed by session and pid. The package never deletes them (they double as
# local audit evidence), but they are useless to anyone but the session that
# wrote them, so they don't belong in history.
.pi/tasks/
.pi/delegate/
.pi/fusion/

# A trial run of a branch keeps its agent directory beside the checkout, so it
# never writes to ~/.pi/agent. It holds sessions and a copied settings file --
# state, not source (see pi/kit/bin/pi-try).
.pi-try/

# Generated colour files: output of bin/theme, rebuilt by one command from
# theme/setups/*.toml. The current-* symlinks in theme/gen are the only state
# the theme system has.
theme/gen/

# Python's bytecode cache. Same rule as sysprobe: build output, rebuilt on
# every run, and a diff you cannot read. Four of these were tracked for a
# while and turned up dirty in `git status` after every theme test.
__pycache__/
